Two notes on what that licence covers, because a software licence over a
repository that also contains a drawing is worth being explicit about.

1. docs/reference/graphite-target.png is the author's own pencil sketch,
   included as a measurement target: the brush engine is judged against its
   alpha distribution, not against an opinion. It is published with the
   author's consent under the same Apache-2.0 terms as the code. It is
   reference material, not a logo or a trade mark.

2. No third-party source has been copied into this repository. Where the
   design was informed by other drawing programs -- Krita and GIMP
   (GPL-3.0), MyPaint (GPL-2.0), and the Android graphics stack -- the
   technique was learned from documentation and published architecture
   descriptions and reimplemented here. docs/phase2-plan.md records which
   idea came from where, and why each was taken, adapted or skipped. See
   "Prior art, and the rule that governs it".

3. app/src/main/assets/brushes and app/src/main/assets/tips hold sixteen
   brush presets and six brush tips converted from the default resource
   bundle that ships with Krita. That bundle's own meta.xml declares

       <meta:meta-userdefined meta:name="license" meta:value="CC-0"/>

   alongside its author line, "Deevad with derivations of the brushes of
   Ramon Miranda, Razvanc, Radian, Wolthera, Storm, Scottyp and other."
   CC-0 is a public domain dedication and places no condition on reuse;
   the attribution above is given because it is deserved, not because it
   is required.

   A preset and the program that reads it are two objects with two
   licences, and conflating them is the mistake this note exists to avoid:
   the data is CC-0, Krita's source is GPL-3.0, and no line of the latter
   is here. tools/brush-picks.txt records exactly which presets were taken
   and tools/krita-brushes.py is the converter, written from the file
   formats rather than from Krita's code. See docs/brushes-plan.md.

Third-party dependencies are declared in gradle/libs.versions.toml and are
retrieved at build time; none is vendored here. The AndroidX libraries this
project builds against are themselves Apache-2.0.
